<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  QBX-232. A TRACK THAT LEAVES A MIXER'S TREE LEAVES THAT MIXER'S SELECTION.

  THE DEFECT. SStdMixer holds its selection in QPointers (selectedTrack_,
  selectedTracks_). A QPointer clears itself when its object is DELETED, which
  is what happens to a track that is plainly removed: nothing re-refs it, the
  count reaches zero and it is deleteLater'd. A track that is MOVED is a
  different story: the destination has already added a ref, so the track stays
  alive and the QPointer never clears. After extract-arrangement the master
  therefore went on selecting a lane that now lives in the arrangement, and
  Track Detail, which follows the master's selection while the Arrangement tab
  is active, showed a lane absent from the arrangement on screen (QBX-231's
  arrangement_track_inserts read path=Drums:1 off the master).

  WHY THE FIX IS NOT IN removeTrack(), where a departure looks like it belongs.
  Two measurements said otherwise, and sections (c) and (d) below are each one
  of them:

    - every mover is remove-THEN-insert (sextractarrangementaction.cpp:227,
      sdissolvearrangementaction.cpp:138, sreparenttrackaction.cpp:115), so
      mid-move the track is in NEITHER tree and "has it left?" has no truthful
      answer yet;
    - a track whose parent is a FOLDER rather than the mixer is detached with
      `delete ownLink` and never reaches removeTrack() at all;
    - selection is TREE-WIDE, because strackpath::pathOf recurses into path
      containers, so a track reparented into a folder of the SAME root never
      leaves the tree and must STAY selected.

  So the question is REACHABILITY, asked once the move has settled:
  SStdMixer::pruneSelectionToTree(), called by extract-arrangement and
  dissolve-arrangement. reparent-track deliberately does not call it, and says
  so at the call site it does not make.

  THE ORACLE. assert-track-selection already reports a selected track that is
  no longer in the mixer's tree as "<detached>" rather than dropping it
  silently, so the broken state is directly assertable and the fixed one is an
  empty set. No new verb, and no assertion that needs the stale pointer
  dereferenced beyond the pathOf walk the verb already does on a live object.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="extract_leaves_master_selection">
  <actions>
    <!-- Track 3 is added clip-free on purpose: it becomes the FOLDER in (c),
         and strackpath addresses a lane's clips and its child lanes in ONE
         index space, so a folder carrying its own clip puts that clip at 0,0
         and the nested lane at 0,1. Measured, after a first attempt indexed a
         clip as though it were the track. Every index below is pinned with an
         assert-track-name at the moment it is used, so a renumbering shows up
         as a name mismatch rather than as a silent wrong target. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="1" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="2" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <set-track-name trackPath="1" name="Mover"/>
    <set-track-name trackPath="2" name="Keep"/>

    <!-- ===== (a) A TOP-LEVEL TRACK LEAVES THE MASTER ===================
         Two tracks selected, and the PRIMARY is the one that will move, so
         both halves are pinned: the mover leaves the set, and the primary
         passes on to who is left (as toggleTrackSelection does). -->
    <select-track trackPath="2"/>
    <select-track trackPath="1" modifiers="ctrl"/>
    <assert-track-selection paths="1;2" primary="1"/>

    <extract-arrangement trackPaths="1" rangeStart="0" rangeEnd="192000"
                         name="Drums" window="range" placeAt="0"/>
    <!-- The asset lane lands where the topmost extracted track was, so the
         master reads [0] original, [1] the Drums lane, [2] Keep. On the
         pre-fix binary this assertion read ["<detached>", "2"] and the primary
         resolved to the empty path. -->
    <assert-track-name trackPath="2" name="Keep"/>
    <assert-track-selection paths="2" primary="2"/>

    <!-- ===== (b) UNDO LEAVES NOTHING DANGLING =========================
         extract-arrangement's inverse is dissolve-arrangement, which moves the
         track back. Restoring the selection is explicitly NOT required; what
         matters is that nothing dangles, so Keep is still the whole selection
         and the returned track is not in it. -->
    <!-- Add the ASSET LANE the extraction just made to the selection, so the
         dissolve has a selected track to REMOVE and its own prune is gated:
         the lane is deleted once it empties (sdissolvearrangementaction.cpp),
         and nothing re-refs it, so its QPointer would clear itself eventually
         but not before the next read. Ctrl, so Keep stays in the set and both
         claims below are live at once. -->
    <select-track trackPath="1" modifiers="ctrl"/>
    <assert-track-selection paths="1;2" primary="1"/>

    <undo/>
    <assert-track-count arrangement="" count="3"/>
    <assert-track-name trackPath="1" name="Mover"/>
    <!-- The asset lane is gone from the set and the primary passed to Keep;
         the returned Mover is NOT selected. Without the dissolve's prune this
         read ["<detached>", "2"]. -->
    <assert-track-selection paths="2" primary="2"/>

    <!-- ===== (c) A NESTED TRACK LEAVES THE MASTER ======================
         THE SHAPE A removeTrack() DROP CANNOT SEE. Mover becomes a child of a
         clip-free folder, so its parent is a folder STrack and not the mixer:
         the extraction detaches it with `delete ownLink` and removeTrack() is
         never called at all. Selecting it first also proves the selection is
         TREE-WIDE, which is what makes (d) a real risk and not a
         hypothetical. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <set-track-name trackPath="3" name="Folder"/>
    <reparent-track source="1" destParent="3" destIndex="-1"/>
    <assert-track-count arrangement="" count="3"/>
    <assert-track-name trackPath="2" name="Folder"/>
    <assert-track-name trackPath="2,0" name="Mover"/>

    <select-track trackPath="2,0"/>
    <assert-track-selection paths="2,0" primary="2,0"/>
    <extract-arrangement trackPaths="2,0" rangeStart="0" rangeEnd="192000"
                         name="Nested" window="range" placeAt="0"/>
    <assert-track-selection paths="" primary=""/>

    <!-- ===== (d) A REPARENT KEEPS THE SELECTION ========================
         THE REGRESSION A removeTrack() DROP WOULD CAUSE. reparent-track moves
         a track WITHIN one root, so it never leaves the mixer's tree and must
         stay selected at its new, nested path. A drop inside removeTrack()
         reads the detach half as a departure and deselects a folder drag. -->
    <assert-track-count arrangement="" count="4"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <set-track-name trackPath="4" name="Dragged"/>
    <set-track-name trackPath="5" name="DropFolder"/>
    <assert-track-count arrangement="" count="6"/>

    <select-track trackPath="4"/>
    <assert-track-selection paths="4" primary="4"/>
    <!-- reparent-track resolves BOTH paths before the detach (measured: (c)'s
         destParent="3" resolved with source="1" still in place), so
         destParent="5" is DropFolder and not what index 5 becomes after the
         move. The detach renumbers the top level, leaving DropFolder at 4. -->
    <reparent-track source="4" destParent="5" destIndex="-1"/>
    <assert-track-count arrangement="" count="5"/>
    <assert-track-name trackPath="4" name="DropFolder"/>
    <assert-track-name trackPath="4,0" name="Dragged"/>
    <!-- STILL SELECTED, at its new nested path. This is the assertion a drop
         inside removeTrack() fails: it sees the detach half, finds the track
         absent from the mixer's children and deselects a track that never left
         the tree. -->
    <assert-track-selection paths="4,0" primary="4,0"/>
  </actions>
</SActionScript>
